<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guided Democracy in Indonesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Demokrasi Terpimpin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guided Democracy was the political system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> from 1959 to 1965. After he restored the 1945 constitution by decree in July 1959, parliament lost much of its power and Sukarno ruled by balancing the army against the Communist Party, under slogans of national unity and continuing revolution. It brought confrontation abroad and economic collapse at home, and ended after the 30 September Movement of 1965 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> to power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guided-democracy-in-indonesia.jpg" alt="Guided Democracy in Indonesia" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Davidelit on en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1959_Sukarno%27s_Presidential_Decree.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The period of liberal democracy, from 1950 to the declaration of martial law in 1957, saw six cabinets rise and fall, and even the first national elections of 1955 brought no stability. After a visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in October 1956, where he was impressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s strong leadership, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> spoke of his konsepsi, a new system of government. In 1957 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permesta">Permesta</a> rebellion began, and in March Sukarno accepted General Abdul Haris Nasution's proposal for nationwide martial law. In April a non-party Working Cabinet under Djuanda Kartawidjaja took office, and a National Council of functional groups followed in May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> suited the village way of deciding questions by long deliberation and consensus under the guidance of elders, and that the president should guide such deliberation at the national level. He set out the ideology in his Independence Day address of 17 August 1957, later named the Manipol, or Political Manifesto, and expanded as USDEK: the 1945 Constitution, Indonesian socialism, guided democracy, a guided economy and Indonesian identity. Nasakom, a blend of nationalism, religion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, was meant to satisfy the army, the secular nationalists, the Islamic groups and the communists. In practice it meant centralised rule, martial law and a great reduction of civil liberties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>On 5 July 1959 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> restored the 1945 Constitution by decree and dissolved the Constitutional Assembly. Masyumi was banned in 1960, and in March of that year he dissolved the legislature after it rejected his budget, replacing it with an appointed house in which the armed forces and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia">Communist Party of Indonesia</a>, the PKI, both gained seats. By 1962 the PKI had over two million members, and the army, through bodies such as Sekber Golkar, formed in October 1964, built up its own political strength. In March 1963 the Provisional People's Consultative Assembly made Sukarno president for life.</p><p>Abroad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-new-guinea">West New Guinea</a> under the New York Agreement of 15 August 1962 and from 1963 waged konfrontasi against Malaysia, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 7 January 1965. At home <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> had reached 100 percent a year by 1960. On the night of 30 September 1965 six generals were kidnapped and murdered by the 30 September Movement; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> quickly crushed it, ending Guided Democracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>'s effective rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guided Democracy in Indonesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1959 Sukarno restored the 1945 constitution by decree and ruled through <b>Guided Democracy</b>, balancing the army against the PKI.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
